46
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:27,560
So, first, a very quick recap of caching.

47
00:03:27,560 --> 00:03:28,780
This is going to be fast.

48
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:33,600
If you find it's too fast, then please just check out the presentation I did two years ago,

49
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:34,600
Practical Webcache Poisoning.

50
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:37,600
It has a slow explanation of these concepts.

51
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:46,600
So, caches work by saving copies of web pages in order to reduce load on the backend system.

52
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:53,600
And web cache poisoning is simply where you trick a cache into saving a harmful page and

53
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:56,140
serving it up to other users.

54
00:03:56,140 --> 00:03:57,140
How do you do that?

55
00:03:57,140 --> 00:04:03,680
Well, caches map requests to responses using something called a cache key, which is a value

56
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:08,060
that's generated from selected components of the request.

57
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:14,380
Normally, the host header and the request line and maybe one or two other headers.

58
00:04:14,380 --> 00:04:20,840
So the key to basic cache poisoning is simply to edit a value that's not in the cache key.

59
00:04:20,840 --> 00:04:27,120
For example, any of the values highlighted in orange in this request.

60
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:32,740
And if you can use those to coax the application into giving a harmful response, then the cache

61
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:36,240
will save that and it will serve it up to other users.

62
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:39,660
So that's basic cache poisoning.

63
00:04:39,660 --> 00:04:46,080
But this approach only goes so far because most websites take most of their input from

64
00:04:46,080 --> 00:04:51,580
the path and the query stream, which are generally included in the cache key and thereby can't

65
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:53,740
be used for cache poisoning.

66
00:04:53,740 --> 00:04:55,580
Or so I thought.

67
00:04:55,580 --> 00:05:00,980
Actually they can't be used for cache poisoning unless someone decides that analytics parameters

68
00:05:00,980 --> 00:05:02,360
are hurting performance.

69
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:06,940
So they need to rewrite the query string before putting it in the key.

70
00:05:06,940 --> 00:05:11,260
Or the cache decides it's going to normalize keys because, you know, normalization is great.

71
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:13,600
Nothing ever goes wrong there.

72
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:19,980
Or cache key components are stored next to each other as unescaped strings.

73
00:05:19,980 --> 00:05:23,300
Or there simply is no cache key.

74
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:27,700
Any of these is going to cause problems.

75
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:29,360
So how do we find these problems?

76
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:35,700
Well, because the way these problems arise is through implementation and configuration issues,

77
00:05:35,700 --> 00:05:39,780
they're different on every single target caching site.

78
00:05:39,780 --> 00:05:47,640
So the first step is to understand how the particular site that we're trying to exploit actually works.

79
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:51,880
And to achieve that, what we need to do is to pick a cache oracle.

80
00:05:51,880 --> 00:05:57,400
This is a page or endpoint on the target site that we're going to ask a series of questions

81
00:05:57,400 --> 00:06:01,920
to in order to understand how the cache works.

82
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:07,020
This page needs to be cacheable and it needs to have some way of us knowing whether we've

83
00:06:07,020 --> 00:06:09,040
got a cache hit or a cache miss.

84
00:06:09,040 --> 00:06:13,260
That could be something really obvious, like a response header that tells us that we had

85
00:06:13,260 --> 00:06:14,380
a hit.

86
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:20,620
Or it could be inferred based on values being reflected back or dynamic content.

87
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:26,140
Or it could be based on inference through the length of time that the server takes to respond

88
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:27,660
to our request.

89
00:06:27,660 --> 00:06:34,820
Because if a response comes from a cache, typically it will come back much faster.

90
00:06:34,820 --> 00:06:41,320
And in theory, if a page has those qualities, that is good enough to be your cache oracle.

91
00:06:41,320 --> 00:06:47,080
But it will make our life a lot easier if this page also reflects the whole URL and reflects

92
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:50,860
at least one specific parameter.

93
00:06:50,860 --> 00:06:56,320
So having chosen our cache oracle, the next step is to get any bonus information that we

94
00:06:56,320 --> 00:06:57,820
can grab hold of.

95
00:06:57,820 --> 00:07:02,080
Like for example, you might be able to find the source code for the cache, some documentation

96
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:06,400
that tells you what the cache key is, or you might find some pragma headers like those

97
00:07:06,400 --> 00:07:10,160
ones we saw on Akamai that just make them tell you the cache key.

98
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:14,720
And if you haven't gathered any extra info, the next step is to ask a series of questions

99
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:19,940
to this oracle to understand how the cache is working.

100
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:27,180
You ask a question to an oracle by issuing two requests to the cache which are very similar

101
00:07:27,180 --> 00:07:31,440
and seeing if the second one gets a cache hit with the first one.

102
00:07:31,440 --> 00:07:38,080
So that tells us whether there are two requests were issued with the same key or not.

103
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:42,120
The things you want to be looking out for are things like are they transforming my request

104
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:44,220
in any way to make this key?

105
00:07:44,220 --> 00:07:46,820
Maybe they're doing a URL decode.

106
00:07:46,820 --> 00:07:48,960
Or maybe they're escaping certain characters.

107
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:53,000
Maybe they're removing particular segments.

108
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:59,040
Or maybe they're doing some kind of URL parsing in order to make those transformations.

109
00:07:59,040 --> 00:08:03,660
After sending a whole bunch of questions and hopefully finding a few interesting quirks in

110
00:08:03,660 --> 00:08:06,060
how the cache key is generated.

111
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:12,760
The next step to building a high severity exploit is to take that behavior and then find a gadget

