176
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:31,520
On a normal dynamic page if you change a query parameter you'll see a difference in the response.

177
00:12:31,520 --> 00:12:35,760
But if they have their cache configured like this you'll just get a cache hit and it won't

178
00:12:35,760 --> 00:12:40,800
even tell you that you've got a cache hit so no matter what changes you make to any query parameters

179
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:46,320
even if you add an extra cache buster you're just going to get the same response and it's really easy

180
00:12:46,320 --> 00:12:52,480
to accidentally think that that response is static because you think you're talking to the back-end

181
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:55,360
server but you're actually just talking to the cache.

182
00:12:56,960 --> 00:13:04,640
So, how do you find these pages? Well, one approach is to put cache busters in a whole bunch of headers that

183
00:13:04,640 --> 00:13:14,320
might be included in the cache key. So, in this example here I've selected headers where I can put a value in

184
00:13:14,320 --> 00:13:18,320
