00:13:25,840 --> 00:13:32,240
This approach works really well on some systems. For example, on every Cloudflare system the origin

187
00:13:32,240 --> 00:13:38,240
is included in the cache key by default. However, there's a couple of issues with it. One is that

188
00:13:38,240 --> 00:13:43,200
on other systems changing the origin will change the application's behavior which is not what we want.

189
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:48,720
And also some caches just don't bother including any of those headers in the key.

190
00:13:48,720 --> 00:13:53,360
So even with all these fancy cache busters and headers you're still going to get a cache hit.

191
00:13:55,040 --> 00:14:00,320
How do you deal with that? Well, there's one approach we can take which is kind of to cheat

192
00:14:00,320 --> 00:14:06,320
and that is to use the purge or fastly purge method. We'll never guess who fastly purge works on.

193
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:15,920
What this does is this HTTP method lets us delete entries from the target's cache with no authentication.

194
00:14:15,920 --> 00:14:23,120
It's fantastic for cache poisoning. It's surprisingly widely supported and it lets us punch holes in the

195
00:14:23,120 --> 00:14:30,000
cache and find out whether the backend page is dynamic or not. However, there's obviously a bit of a

196
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:35,520
race condition when you use that method because you have to send your follow-up request immediately

197
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:40,240
afterwards and if someone else gets in there you'll get their cache response. So it's not ideal for

198
00:14:40,240 --> 00:14:47,120
automation. And as usual, it doesn't always work. It doesn't work on every system out there. That would

199
00:14:47,120 --> 00:14:57,040
be too easy. So another approach you can take is to take advantage of normalization. So caches generally

200
00:14:57,040 --> 00:15:05,200
don't do much or any normalization on the path that they see in the request and the path is virtually

201
00:15:05,200 --> 00:15:11,360
always included in the key. But backend servers typically do do a bit of processing on the path.

