that by sending a request to trigger this redirect with a really long query string such that the request

223
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:44,720
line that's the path and the query string and the method taken together, all fill up the maximum

224
00:17:44,720 --> 00:17:52,000
allowable length of request line that server will tolerate. So what happens after we send that? Well,

225
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:57,520
as long as that response gets cached, which Cloudflare is happy to do, when someone else tries to access the

226
00:17:57,520 --> 00:18:04,560
login page, they're going to get this redirect. And they're going to follow the redirect. But in the

227
00:18:04,560 --> 00:18:09,120
process of following it, because the server sticking this extra forward slash on the end of the path,

228
00:18:09,840 --> 00:18:16,480
the request line gets one byte longer. And that puts it over the maximum length that the server

229
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:22,160
supports and it will just reject the request. So with one request, we've persistently blocked access

230
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:29,680
to Cloudflare's login page. It's worth mentioning here that we couldn't simply get that get that final

231
00:18:29,680 --> 00:18:36,080
414 response cache directly because Cloudflare would refuse to cache a response that has an error

232
00:18:36,080 --> 00:18:43,840
status code like that. It's the redirect that gives this attack a layer of indirection that makes it

233
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:53,840
possible. Another benefit of cacheable redirects is that they let you inject poisoned parameters onto the

234
00:18:53,840 --> 00:19:00,960
redirect destination, even if the redirect destination itself is not cacheable. So local redirects are

235
00:19:00,960 --> 00:19:09,120
really useful in general with cache poisoning. So I reported this to Cloudflare and it actually gave them

236
00:19:09,120 --> 00:19:14,720
a bit of an interesting problem because they could easily fix it on their own site just by changing the

237
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:23,680
redirects behavior, right? But what about all of Cloudflare's clients? Because this is a common redirect

238
00:19:23,680 --> 00:19:29,600
style. So Cloudflare could patch it for themselves, but all their clients would still be vulnerable. So they

239
00:19:29,600 --> 00:19:35,120
had to come up with a general purpose mitigation. And that's what they did. They wrote something that's

240
00:19:35,120 --> 00:19:43,600
basically said, if the query string is not included in the cache key, and we get a redirect response where

241
00:19:43,600 --> 00:19:52,080
the query string in the redirect matches exactly the query string in the request, then don't cache

242
00:19:52,800 --> 00:19:58,800
that redirect. And unfortunately, I was able to bypass that by URL encoding one character

243
00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:05,200
in the query string, thereby meaning that the query string didn't match the query string in the response.

244
00:20:05,200 --> 00:20:10,640
And Cloudflare have now fixed that. But obviously, if there's any other transformations happening in

245
00:20:10,640 --> 00:20:16,400
between the query string, the request, and the query string in the response, you'll be able to once again,

246
00:20:16,400 --> 00:20:25,440
bypass this mitigation and take stuff down. Now, at this point, we've seen how some quite simple

247
00:20:25,440 --> 00:20:33,120
transformations can have major unexpected side effects. But we'll also find that in some cases,

248
00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:38,880
the transformations themselves can get really quite messy, especially with a bit of encouragement from a

249
00:20:38,880 --> 00:20:47,600
hacker. And the main reason this happens is because the site's decided to exclude a specific query parameter

250
00:20:47,600 --> 00:20:55,600
from the cache key. I've seen this happen most often with analytics parameters like UTM content.

251
00:20:55,600 --> 00:21:02,240
So you can see the reasoning here, right? Something like UTM content is not intended to be used by the

252
00:21:02,240 --> 00:21:06,880
backend server. It's only intended to be used by client-side JavaScript. So surely it's harmless

253
00:21:07,520 --> 00:21:13,840
to exclude it from the key. Well, obviously, if you've got a full URL reflection based vulnerability,

254
00:21:13,840 --> 00:21:20,560
you can use it to exploit that with cache poisoning, which is wonderful. But what if you want to exploit

255
00:21:21,440 --> 00:21:28,160
a gadget in a different parameter? Say the target is excluding UTM content from the cache key, and you

256
00:21:28,160 --> 00:21:37,920
want to put a payload in the term parameter. Is that possible? Well, in theory, it's not. But as usual,

257
00:21:37,920 --> 00:21:45,200
in practice, things go wrong, and you'll find it often is. It all depends how things get implemented.

258
00:21:45,840 --> 00:21:50,720
And well, you'll generally find some people will take my approach to implementing things.

259
00:21:51,520 --> 00:21:58,400
They'll Google it, land on Stack Overflow, and end up using a regex. Given a regex like this,

260
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:04,800
that tries to exclude the query parameter called underscore from the cache key, how can we place a

261
00:22:04,800 --> 00:22:13,280
payload in the queue parameter here? Well, the regex isn't anchored properly. There's an assumption

262
00:22:13,280 --> 00:22:17,440
that there's no equals in the query string, other than the one that starts the query string. So we

263
00:22:17,440 --> 00:22:26,160
can just kind of squeeze the payload in like so. Now, with this specific regex, because they're doing

264
00:22:26,160 --> 00:22:32,560
a substitution and inserting this query string where the matched parameter was, we can actually

265
00:22:32,560 --> 00:22:40,480
only inject our payload to anywhere in the URL where there's already a question mark. These kind of

266
00:22:40,480 --> 00:22:47,520
substitutions can make this type of issue really hard to find and also put some interesting caveats

267
00:22:47,520 --> 00:22:55,600
on what you can and can't exploit. And here, once again, I've targeted a soft target to begin with,

268
00:22:55,600 --> 00:23:03,440
but it also works on some more serious players. For example, Akamai. Remember how we got Akamai to show

269
00:23:03,440 --> 00:23:10,240
us its cache keys earlier, and some of them had this Akamai transform parameter, and some of them didn't.

270
00:23:10,240 --> 00:23:17,520
That made me think, well, maybe they're excluding this parameter from the key. And sure enough, if we

271
00:23:17,520 --> 00:23:24,320
specify this parameter on a request to any Akamai site, you'll find it doesn't get reflected back to

272
00:23:24,320 --> 00:23:30,000
you in the X true cache key header. And if you change the value, you'll get a cache hit. So it's clearly

273
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:40,640
being excluded from the key. And thanks to Akamai's suboptimal URL parsing, you can actually change the

274
00:23:40,640 --> 00:23:49,280
ampersand in that request to a question mark and thereby append a cloaked value to an arbitrary

275
00:23:49,280 --> 00:23:56,160
parameter in the request. And that will not appear in the key. So you can use this to append arbitrary

276
00:23:56,160 --> 00:24:03,920
malicious values to arbitrary parameters on Akamai systems. However, there is a catch, which is that

277
00:24:03,920 --> 00:24:10,400
there's an invisible cache key bit, which is not in any of the cache keys that get shown to you. But you can

278
00:24:10,400 --> 00:24:16,880
tell it's there from the behavior. And the bit is set if the initial request contains the Akamai

279
00:24:16,880 --> 00:24:24,240
transform parameter. So what that means is, this technique is really powerful, but it only works

280
00:24:24,240 --> 00:24:32,240
on sites that have already either already using Akamai transform. I've reported this to Akamai and

281
00:24:32,240 --> 00:24:39,440
they'd like me to tell you that they are working on a fix. Now, on a different target, something was

282
00:24:39,440 --> 00:24:47,200
behaving really weird and I couldn't find a cache key oracle that was any good. So instead, I managed

283
00:24:47,200 --> 00:24:55,040
to get hold of the code for the cache. And on their homepage, they suggest filtering out parameters for

284
00:24:55,040 --> 00:25:02,880
where the name starts with the UTM underscore. So this is a great candidate for cache parameter cloaking,

285
00:25:02,880 --> 00:25:08,640
as long as we can bypass their query parsing. So if we look at their query parsing,

286
00:25:09,920 --> 00:25:14,240
I found that they're, they're splitting parameters, they're splitting the query, they're splitting

287
00:25:14,240 --> 00:25:20,640
parameters on semicolons, as well as ampersands. And obviously everyone knows you're only meant to

288
00:25:20,640 --> 00:25:25,520
split parameters on ampersand. So I thought, great, I'll use this for my live demo, I'll have an exploit that

289
00:25:25,520 --> 00:25:33,680
looks something like this, and I'll be able to poison arbitrary values. And I set it up for my demo. And then I tested it,

290
00:25:33,680 --> 00:25:43,200
and it didn't work. And it turns out the reason it doesn't work is because this cache is a plugin for Rails and Rails itself

291
00:25:43,200 --> 00:25:54,320
also splits parameters on semicolons, as well as ampersands. Now this is great news for Rack cache, although I still exploit it in a different way later.

292
00:25:55,200 --> 00:26:03,520
But it's bad news for Rails. Because what it means is that if you use Rails with any normal caching system, like

293
00:26:03,520 --> 00:26:11,120
Varnish or Akamai or whatever, and you eliminate any parameter from the cache key, then an attacker can exploit that

294
00:26:11,120 --> 00:26:21,280
to make arbitrary changes to arbitrary parameters. So taking this JSONP example here, if the target cache is

295
00:26:21,280 --> 00:26:28,320
excluding the UTM content parameter, as far as the cache is concerned, there's one parameter, and that's called

296
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:36,640
callback. And the value of that is whatever the attacker chooses. But when Rails parses this, they see

297
00:26:36,640 --> 00:26:43,040
three parameters, they see callback, they see UTM content, which is not relevant, and they see an extra

298
00:26:43,040 --> 00:26:49,040
callback parameter. And because that one's last, they prioritize it. So an attacker can put some malicious

299
00:26:49,040 --> 00:26:56,800
JavaScript in there. And as a result, take full control over every page that uses this particular JSONP

300
00:26:56,800 --> 00:26:57,200
gadget.

301
00:26:57,200 --> 00:27:06,880
Another way of hiding parameters from the cache key is simply to send a post request. This is a bit

302
00:27:06,880 --> 00:27:13,600
ridiculous. I didn't really think this was going to work, but it does. So hey, here we are. So I found an

303
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:21,920
online mapping service where they had XSS via a post parameter, and you had to bypass a WAF, but it wasn't

304
00:27:21,920 --> 00:27:28,480
that hard. And the cool thing was, yeah, the query, the method wasn't in the cache key. So having sent this

305
00:27:28,480 --> 00:27:35,040
request, if someone then fetched that page, which is what you're meant to do with that page, then my

306
00:27:35,040 --> 00:27:42,240
payload would come back. And once again, I had control over every site, every page on that site, persistently.

307
00:27:43,520 --> 00:27:50,720
Interestingly, I actually got a research collision with someone else with this topic. So he found this

