00:27:50,720 --> 00:27:56,480
technique independently at around the same time as me. And he's just published a blog post on it. So

309
00:27:56,480 --> 00:28:04,880
please go check that out as well. Now, with a small tweak, we can take that approach and make it work

310
00:28:04,880 --> 00:28:11,600
on loads more systems. How can you do that? Well, there's a clue in the varnish documentation.

311
00:28:11,600 --> 00:28:23,920
It says, if it gets a request with the body, it will pass the body onto the backend. And the default

312
00:28:23,920 --> 00:28:29,520
configuration file removes the body for get requests, because get requests because it's

313
00:28:29,520 --> 00:28:38,560
questionable. If a get with the body is valid, but some applications use it. So what happens if you

314
00:28:38,560 --> 00:28:44,800
don't have this snippet that removes the body from get requests in your varnish config, and you're using

315
00:28:44,800 --> 00:28:55,040
a framework like Rails, which supports parameters in the body of get requests? Well, GitHub found out.

316
00:28:56,080 --> 00:29:03,280
Thanks to this behavior, I could poison arbitrary parameters with arbitrary values on every cacheable

317
00:29:03,280 --> 00:29:11,760
page on GitHub. With this example payload here, what I'm doing is I'm poisoning the report abuse page.

318
00:29:11,760 --> 00:29:19,920
So if you try and report abuse on my profile, it ends up reporting somebody else. Also by poisoning

319
00:29:19,920 --> 00:29:28,960
other pages, I could do things like apply arbitrary filters to searches, completely disable access to

320
00:29:28,960 --> 00:29:36,640
topics on any pages that I don't like. I disable access to static files in any repositories that I don't like,

321
00:29:37,200 --> 00:29:45,840
and so on. This got a seven and a half K bounty off GitHub, which was pretty cool. I've decided so

322
00:29:45,840 --> 00:29:54,160
because the term get request with a body is quite cumbersome, I've decided to name that type of request a fat get

323
00:29:54,160 --> 00:30:04,560
request. Now this technique actually works on quite a few systems. In addition to working on varnish systems

324
00:30:04,560 --> 00:30:10,640
that don't have that little snippet that deletes the body, it also works on quite a few people using

325
00:30:11,600 --> 00:30:19,680
Cloudflare because because Cloudflare will always pass the body of fat get requests onto the backend.

326
00:30:19,680 --> 00:30:29,680
And I told Cloudflare about this. And they updated their documentation to say do not trust get request bodies,

327
00:30:29,680 --> 00:30:35,680
which seems quite strange to me because what they're saying is we're always going to give you this value,

328
00:30:35,680 --> 00:30:39,680
but you should never use this value under any circumstances because it's dangerous.

329
00:30:39,680 --> 00:30:47,680
So I went, okay, great. Now I can exploit lots of sites using Cloudflare. And one of the sites I can exploit is the very

330
00:30:47,680 --> 00:30:57,680
site where this documentation was hosted because this is hosted on support.cloudflare.com. And that is built on Zendesk.

331
00:30:57,680 --> 00:31:11,680
And Zendesk supports, it's in Zendesk users Cloudflare and supports fat get requests. So by issuing this request to anyone using Zendesk, I can poison their login page. So that if a user

332
00:31:11,680 --> 00:31:23,680
browsers to the login page and presses login, they go through an invisible series of redirects that logs them out and then effectively logs them back into my account invisibly.

333
00:31:23,680 --> 00:31:31,680
So if they then happen to file a support ticket on the target that's filed in my account, and I can just log in and retrieve it later.

334
00:31:31,680 --> 00:31:52,680
Obviously, you can also take down most of the site as well as usual. And well, yeah, so that's cool. And that works on that core issue will work on basically everything that uses rails behind Cloudflare, as well as various other frameworks.

335
00:31:52,680 --> 00:32:05,680
There's an interesting side point here, though, you might have noticed that although this fat get parameter, this fat get approach to parameter cloaking is really powerful, lets us make arbitrary changes to arbitrary parameters.

336
00:32:05,680 --> 00:32:14,680
The impact of the two examples I've shown you on Zendesk and GitHub has not been as bad as some of the earlier vulnerabilities.

337
00:32:14,680 --> 00:32:22,680
And the reason for that is that basically the impact comes down to what gadgets you can find on the target site.

338
00:32:22,680 --> 00:32:32,680
And on those sites in the time constraints I had, I found that were quite hardened, and it was difficult to find a gadget that could prove a really high impact like full site takeover.

339
00:32:32,680 --> 00:32:46,680
So identifying useful gadgets is the key to high impact cash poisoning exploits with these techniques.

340
00:32:46,680 --> 00:32:57,680
So what other gadgets are available to us? Well, we've kind of seen that JSONP is useful, but in general, any dynamic content in a JavaScript file or in a CSS file.

341
00:32:57,680 --> 00:33:05,680
Normally, these are both completely harmless because if you point a browser at those directly, then the browser won't execute it.

342
00:33:05,680 --> 00:33:18,680
But with this, but with cache poisoning, dynamic content in resource files becomes extremely powerful and generally lets us take over any pages that reference those resource files.

343
00:33:18,680 --> 00:33:31,680
With JavaScript, obviously you get full page takeover and with CSS, you can exfiltrate arbitrary content from the page, including invisible content like cross-site request forgery tokens.

344
00:33:31,680 --> 00:33:41,680
In fact, if the page importing a CSS file doesn't have a doc type, then the CSS file doesn't even need to be real CSS.

345
00:33:41,680 --> 00:33:45,680
And because the content type doesn't need to be text slash CSS.

346
00:33:45,680 --> 00:34:02,680
What you can potentially do in this scenario is you can send a long query or one with a payload in some kind of manner that makes the server give you a server error or a WAF message back that reflects your input.

347
00:34:02,680 --> 00:34:12,680
And because of the lack of the doctype, the browser will just parse this whole HTML response, will just walk through it until it finds valid CSS and then execute it.

348
00:34:12,680 --> 00:34:17,680
So using that, you can potentially even exploit static CSS files.

349
00:34:17,680 --> 00:34:32,680
