00:34:57,680 --> 00:35:00,680
So you'd think it would be fairly secure.

354
00:35:00,680 --> 00:35:08,680
But if you look at the documentation for proxy pass, there's a kind of a tiny clue that there might be something wrong here.

355
00:35:08,680 --> 00:35:20,680
It says if you use proxy pass without a URI, like Mozilla has done, then the request URI is passed to the server in the same form as sent by a client.

356
00:35:20,680 --> 00:35:25,680
So what that means is it's passed to the backend without any normalization.

357
00:35:25,680 --> 00:35:32,680
And the value stored in the proxy cache key is normalized.

358
00:35:32,680 --> 00:35:35,680
So this can cause problems.

359
00:35:35,680 --> 00:35:46,680
Whenever you open Firefox and periodically thereafter, one of the most important things it does is it checks to see if there's any updates available for itself,

