407
00:40:46,680 --> 00:40:54,680
And yeah, they've now patched this, but I never knew how this attack was actually possible.

408
00:40:54,680 --> 00:41:00,680
We'll never know. But at least I got a T-shirt out of it.

409
00:41:00,680 --> 00:41:08,680
Hey. On the opposite end of the spectrum, some attacks are so practical that it's not possible to do them safely.

410
00:41:08,680 --> 00:41:11,680
I found this out in quite an unpleasant manner.

411
00:41:11,680 --> 00:41:22,680
I was testing a classic cache poisoning technique with a potentially unkeyed header on a specific blog post on Adobe's blog.

412
00:41:22,680 --> 00:41:32,680
And I suddenly got a flood of a large amount of traffic coming to my collaborator server, like really a lot of traffic.

413
00:41:32,680 --> 00:41:40,680
And this didn't make much sense because I was targeting an obscure blog post and I had a URL query based cache buster as well.

414
00:41:40,680 --> 00:41:43,680
And it looked like the query stream was in the cache key.

415
00:41:43,680 --> 00:41:50,680
What it turned out was, yeah, the query stream wasn't the cache key, but they had multiple layers of caching.

416
00:41:50,680 --> 00:41:53,680
And they also had an internal cache.

417
00:41:53,680 --> 00:41:57,680
And the internal cache was saving fragments of responses.

418
00:41:57,680 --> 00:42:00,680
And basically I'd poisoned every fragment.

419
00:42:00,680 --> 00:42:08,680
And so if you loaded any page on this site, then you would get all your resource loads would come from my server.

420
00:42:08,680 --> 00:42:15,680
And every link to every one of their blog posts on that page would also point to my server.

421
00:42:15,680 --> 00:42:17,680
And this was on every page on their site, including their homepage.

422
00:42:17,680 --> 00:42:21,680
So in effect, I was just getting all of the traffic from everyone on their entire site.

423
00:42:21,680 --> 00:42:27,680
And I couldn't undo it. There was no way to disable, to unpoison their cache.

424
00:42:27,680 --> 00:42:29,680
So I panicked a little bit.

425
00:42:29,680 --> 00:42:31,680
I turned off the collaborator server.

426
00:42:31,680 --> 00:42:35,680
So at least I wasn't getting any traffic exactly.

427
00:42:35,680 --> 00:42:40,680
And I notified their security team who were able to fix it in 20 minutes.

428
00:42:40,680 --> 00:42:44,680
And luckily for me, they were quite understanding.

429
00:42:44,680 --> 00:42:48,680
But you can see this kind of outcome is really not great.

430
00:42:48,680 --> 00:42:56,680
It makes the distinction between a legitimate-ish researcher and an attacker really quite blurry.

431
00:42:56,680 --> 00:43:03,680
So I think it's worth seriously asking if you go looking for this type of vulnerability deliberately.

432
00:43:03,680 --> 00:43:09,680
Are you actually doing a service to the company or are you not?

